{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/statusview at exact clean head 7e5416591aed9b9ce31db7f04b0a894bde7708ff, succeeding 0d87b56b. Companion to the cmd/gs artifact 806c884d and the docs artifact at this same head; all three must be co-signed together. It replaces my own 18716a65, which named the dead head fd79bd7f and is retired separately rather than rested on, so the retirement cannot propagate into its successor.\n\nTHE SPLIT IS HERE, AND IT IS STRUCTURAL. ArtifactQuery is the published input contract for the resident's /v0/artifact-query and for the MCP artifacts tool, and it is restored to paths, limit and cursor — I diffed the declaration against main and it is identical. ArtifactSelection is new and CLI-only, carrying State and Reaches. BuildArtifactPage takes the wire type and builds a live-only selection from it; BuildArtifactSelectionPage takes the CLI type; both hand off to one buildArtifactPage that owns the filtering, rows, pagination and cursor. Condition 2 asked that the CLI reach the existing query through one shared implementation rather than a fork, and this is that, minus the struct sharing that leaked the selectors onto two remote surfaces.\n\nWHY THIS SHAPE IS STRONGER THAN A TEST, which is what I checked rather than assumed. Re-adding State and Reaches to ArtifactQuery does not reopen the defect: the fields sit dead, because BuildArtifactPage constructs its selection with ArtifactStateLive and no anchor and never reads them. I ran exactly that mutation twice, expecting the guard tests to go red, and they stayed green — my mutation was wrong, not the tests. The defect only returns when BuildArtifactPage honours the fields, and under that mutation the MCP boundary test fails at once with \"undeclared MCP selector map[state:retired] changed artifact semantics\" and MatchingTotal 0 against a contract of 1. So there are two independent barriers: the wire type carries nothing to honour, and if someone reconnects it the test names what broke.\n\nTHE OTHER TWO ADDITIONS. BuildStalenessWave answers the runbook's whole-log measurement — a forward walk from the anchor artifacts along every Rests-On edge of every effective record, excluding supersede-to-target edges, which is a different direction and a different edge set from the backward artifact-to-artifact walk that Reaches uses. Keeping them apart matters: conflating them would give a number that looks plausible and answers a different question. And the review gate's ApprovedHeads is now the bounded display sample only, with ActionableApprovedHeads exposing the complete deduplicated population for the caller that asks Git.\n\nTHAT GATE CHANGE CLOSES A DEFECT I FILED AGAINST AN EARLIER VERSION OF IT. In assert 747ab00e I measured 58 unique actionable approved heads against a hard ceiling of 50 and showed Quiet() would be false on every possible invocation, making gs reviews permanently red. Separating display from decision fixes the cause rather than the symptom, because the classifier now sees every head and an already-landed one clears regardless of how many historical approvals precede it. The population under that filter grows monotonically, so any design that capped the decision would have failed eventually even if it passed today.\n\nSECURITY: read-only selection throughout, over a projection the caller can already fetch whole. Nothing is signed, admitted or refused differently. The two boundary-relevant properties both improved: the remote input contract is narrower than it was an hour ago, and BuildReviewGate still fails closed on references it cannot resolve, reporting unresolved as a number rather than dropping rows — which matters because its output can release an irreversible migration.\n\nARCHITECTURE: no layer contract moves. This is projection-layer selection beside the builders that already do it. The resident, MCP and HTTP surfaces are byte-for-byte the contract they were before this lane, which is what the requester's ruling 56565ba9 required.\n\nGATES at this exact head, run by me: go vet exit 0; go build exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 27 packages ok; go test -race ./... -count=1 exit 0, no data race; gofmt empty; git diff --check exit 0; worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"7e5416591aed9b9ce31db7f04b0a894bde7708ff","path":"internal/statusview"}}